### 9. Collectors

Collect stream results into a collection or another result.

```
List<Integer> even = nums.stream()
    .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

Result: `[2, 4]`

Memory trick: `collect()` gathers results.

### 10. Map API enhancements

New methods make map operations easier.

```
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.putIfAbsent("A", 10);
map.getOrDefault("B", 0);
map.merge("A", 5, Integer::sum);
```

After these operations, `A` maps to `15`, and `B` returns the default value `0`.

Memory trick: `putIfAbsent` = add if missing; `getOrDefault` = fallback; `merge` = combine values.

### 11. `forEach()`

Perform an action for each element.

```
List<String> names = List.of("Amit", "Ravi");

names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(name));
```

Memory trick: `forEach()` = action on every element.

### 12. Other Java 8 features worth knowing

- Repeating annotations: Apply the same annotation more than once.
- Type annotations: Apply annotations to type usages.
- Base64 API: Encode and decode Base64 data.
- Nashorn JavaScript engine: Run JavaScript through a Java-provided engine (later removed in Java 15).
- PermGen removal: Removed the old Permanent Generation memory area; class metadata moved to native memory via Metaspace.

These are documented in Oracle's [Java 8 release notes](https://www.oracle.com/java/technologies/javase/8-whats-new.html).&#x20;

